# Prefer process.exitCode over process.exit()

`process.exit()` sets the status and terminates immediately. Pending stdout can be dropped. Never call `process.exit()`. Application code returns a result. Only the entry file sets `process.exitCode` and returns.

```typescript
type RunResult =
    | { readonly type: 'success' }
    | { readonly type: 'failure'; readonly message: string; readonly exitCode: 1 };

// ❌ Application kills the process. Tests cannot assert the outcome.
async function run(): Promise<void> {
    process.exit(1);
}

// ✅ Inner code returns a result. src/main.ts records the status.
function toFailure(message: string): RunResult {
    return { type: 'failure', message, exitCode: 1 };
}

async function main(result: RunResult): Promise<void> {
    if (result.type === 'failure') {
        console.error(result.message);
        process.exitCode = result.exitCode;
        return;
    }
}
```
